course: dictionary
lesson: 5933
title: Marvellously and how words show feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Marvellously is an adverb. An adverb tells you how something happens, or how much. Read this: «As dawn comes, no birds sang as they were wont to do, and all was marvellously still.» The word marvellously tells you how still it was: very, extremely still.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. «All was very still» still makes sense, so marvellously means very or extremely here. It can also mean in a wonderful, excellent way, as in «marvellously happy».
explain: Words like marvellously carry warmth. In British stories and speech, people often say things are marvellous when they are pleased. Toad often says things are marvellous, and it tells the reader that something went really well.
fact: Marvellously is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens or how much.
fact: «Marvellously» means in a wonderful, excellent way, or very and extremely.
fact: British spelling is marvellously with two l's; US spelling is marvelously with one.
fact: «Wont» means used to, so «no birds sang as they were wont to do» means no birds sang as they usually did.
fact: A good check is to swap in «very»: «all was very still» still makes sense, so marvellously means very here.
exercise: «The night was marvellously still, with no wind at all.» What does marvellously mean here? | Sadly, with a frown | Loudly, with a crash | *Very, extremely | Stones fell down
exercise: Which word could replace marvellously in «marvellously happy» with a similar meaning? | Slowly | Sadly | Quietly | *Very
exercise: Why does the spelling of marvellously differ between countries? | Because one of them is a shorter word | Because US spelling is always the correct one | *British spelling has two l's, and US spelling has one | Because it is a different word in each country
test: «The garden was marvellously quiet after the storm.» What does marvellously mean here? | Badly | Sadly | Noisily | *Very, extremely
test: Mia says the meal was marvellously good. Which meaning fits best? | In a short, rude way | *In a wonderful, excellent way | Slowly and carefully | Without any food
exam: «The owl sat marvellously still on the branch.» Which word could replace marvellously and keep the meaning? | Sadly | Noisily | Quickly | *Very
